{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 50, :comments ["<b>Before the year of famine came.</b> From here it is forbidden to attend to one's needs in years of famine. Accordingly, it should have said 'And to Joseph were born two sons in the year of plenty' — and there is a support for the words of Beit Shammai, who say (Yevamot 61b) that if a person has two sons he has fulfilled the commandment of פְּרִיָּה וּרְבִיָּה. And had Scripture not told us that two sons were born to him before the year of famine came, I would have said that this is why he named the second son Ephraim — 'for Elohim has made me fruitful' — because he was born in the year of famine, when the whole world was starving and Joseph had grain and fruit in abundance. But now that he called him Ephraim before the year of famine, he surely called him so because at that point he had fulfilled the commandment of פְּרִיָּה וּרְבִיָּה — this is what he said, 'for Elohim has made me fruitful,' after having two sons, according to the words of Beit Shammai. However, according to Beit Hillel, who hold it must be specifically a son and a daughter, one cannot explain it thus. And it is possible that there is a hint from here to what is written, that a person should not have relations when he is hungry nor when he is sated (Rambam, Laws of De'ot 4:19); therefore it does not say 'in the year of plenty,' for it speaks of the intermediate time between plenty and famine."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The phrase ויש כדמות סמך (literally 'and there is a likeness of support') is rendered as 'and there is a support'; the reading is textually uncertain but the sense is preserved."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:56.938606748Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}